      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 751,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 581,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113913,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 347,
        "headingId": 581,
        "headingText": "<p>The most common location is the posterolateral hard palate, but it can occur in other minor salivary sites, including the tongue, oral floor, lacrimal glands, upper airway, proximal oesophagus, trachea, and bronchi {1923419; 3083385; 7741108; 19948640; 23793784; 24863247; 26634609}. Major salivary gland involvement is rare.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-11-07T11:42:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 752,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 582,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113914,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 347,
        "headingId": 582,
        "headingText": "<p>The classic palatal presentation is a single lateral ulcer near the junction of the hard and soft palate. Bilateral or metachronous lesions may rarely occur. Swelling is typical and may precede the development of a crater-like ulcer ranging in size from 10&nbsp;to 15&nbsp;mm, which clinically can mimic a malignancy {30776173}. Most cases are asymptomatic; a burning sensation, mild pain, or numbness is occasionally identified {1923419}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-11-07T11:43:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 753,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 583,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113915,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 347,
        "headingId": 583,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Necrotizing sialometaplasia typically presents in the fourth decade of life, with a male predilection {1923419}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:50:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 754,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 584,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113916,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 347,
        "headingId": 584,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The etiology of necrotizing sialometaplasia is uncertain, but it is probably associated with trauma, vascular compromise, and/or hypoxia {23837804}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:50:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 755,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 585,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113917,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 347,
        "headingId": 585,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The pathogenesis involves vascular compromise leading to ischaemic infarction of seromucous glands and subsequent regeneration {12049830}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:50:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 762,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 592,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113918,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 347,
        "headingId": 592,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Necrotizing sialometaplasia appears as an ulcer 10&ndash;15&nbsp;mm in diameter {1923419}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:50:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 756,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 586,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113919,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 347,
        "headingId": 586,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pre-existing salivary lobular architecture is usually preserved {19415943}. Early lesions display acinar infarction/necrosis accompanied by mucus spillage. Ulceration varies. As lesions mature, involved ducts and acini undergo squamous metaplasia. Generally, cytological features remain bland, but keratinocytic regenerative atypia with mitotic activity may be present. Remnants of ductal cells, interspersed mucocytes, granulation reaction, and stromal subacute inflammation may comingle. Recognition is most challenging when lobular preservation is poor, or when pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia of the intact overlying mucosa merges with ductal squamous metaplasia, simulating squamous carcinoma {4716764; 19415943}. Differential diagnosis should include subacute necrotizing sialadenitis {10807718; 17142073}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:50:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 757,
